WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.335 --> 00:00:02.628
- It's important to listen to everyone,

00:00:02.628 --> 00:00:04.844
as many people as you
can and get their input.

00:00:04.844 --> 00:00:07.103
TISCH graduates, you made it.

00:00:07.103 --> 00:00:09.936
(audience cheers)

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:13.056
And, you're (bleep).

00:00:13.056 --> 00:00:15.422
You can't please everybody, all the time,

00:00:15.422 --> 00:00:16.619
or even part of the time.

00:00:16.619 --> 00:00:18.643
I just try to do my best at

00:00:18.643 --> 00:00:20.443
doing the right thing, if you will.

00:00:20.443 --> 00:00:23.926
If you don't go, you'll never know.

00:00:23.926 --> 00:00:27.786
Sometimes you need that,
from an outside source

00:00:27.786 --> 00:00:30.000
in order to stimulate you and get you

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.558
inspired almost, revved up
about doing something different.

00:00:34.558 --> 00:00:36.170
- He's an American actor and producer,

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:38.900
who has starred in over 100 films.

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:40.606
He earned an academy award for portraying

00:00:40.606 --> 00:00:44.267
Jake Lamotta, in the
1980 film, "Raging Bull."

00:00:44.267 --> 00:00:45.669
His business ventures include,

00:00:45.669 --> 00:00:48.316
co-founding the film
studio, Tribeca Productions,

00:00:48.316 --> 00:00:50.367
as well as the Tribeca Film Festival.

00:00:50.367 --> 00:00:52.614
He's Robert Deniro, and here's my take

00:00:52.614 --> 00:00:54.604
on his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:54.604 --> 00:00:56.061
Rule number three, is
my personal favorite,

00:00:56.061 --> 00:00:58.041
and make sure to stick around
all the way to the end,

00:00:58.041 --> 00:01:00.458
for some special bonus clips.

00:01:03.521 --> 00:01:06.179
- Personally, I always try to listen,

00:01:06.179 --> 00:01:09.666
because maybe I'm not getting something.

00:01:09.666 --> 00:01:11.429
I always try and take everybody's,

00:01:11.429 --> 00:01:13.814
because it's important
to listen to everyone,

00:01:13.814 --> 00:01:14.931
as many people as you can

00:01:14.931 --> 00:01:16.178
and get their input.

00:01:16.178 --> 00:01:18.242
But, then finally, you have
to make your own decision

00:01:18.242 --> 00:01:20.701
about what you're going
to do and that's it.

00:01:20.701 --> 00:01:22.377
That's what you feel and that's it.

00:01:22.377 --> 00:01:24.956
And, that's the most important thing,

00:01:24.956 --> 00:01:28.031
I think the most important thing,

00:01:28.031 --> 00:01:30.551
because that's where
the uniqueness comes in,

00:01:30.551 --> 00:01:32.116
because it's you, you've done it,

00:01:32.116 --> 00:01:33.448
it's your thing.

00:01:33.448 --> 00:01:35.764
It's not anybody else's, it's your choice.

00:01:35.764 --> 00:01:37.472
- [Interviewer] And, when
young actors come to you

00:01:37.472 --> 00:01:39.500
looking for advice, what do you tell them?

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:42.042
- I tell them, just to keep at it,

00:01:42.042 --> 00:01:44.930
and not be you know, discouraged by,

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:47.604
if you're going for say,
readings and stuff like that,

00:01:47.604 --> 00:01:49.609
you have to try, always try your best,

00:01:49.609 --> 00:01:52.568
and just follow your instincts more,

00:01:52.568 --> 00:01:56.079
so you can be free and easy about it,

00:01:56.079 --> 00:01:58.662
and assume that you're
not going to get it,

00:01:58.662 --> 00:02:01.746
so you have a more, "devil
may care," attitude about it.

00:02:01.746 --> 00:02:03.475
I think that helps, a lot.

00:02:03.475 --> 00:02:07.211
Thank you for inviting me
to celebrate with you today.

00:02:07.211 --> 00:02:09.614
TISCH graduates, you made it.

00:02:09.614 --> 00:02:12.447
(audience cheers)

00:02:13.687 --> 00:02:15.591
And, you're (bleep).

00:02:15.591 --> 00:02:18.424
(audience cheers)

00:02:26.499 --> 00:02:27.916
Think about that.

00:02:29.133 --> 00:02:31.075
The graduates from the college of nursing,

00:02:31.075 --> 00:02:32.658
they all have jobs.

00:02:33.722 --> 00:02:36.291
The graduates from the
college of dentistry,

00:02:36.291 --> 00:02:37.903
fully employed.

00:02:37.903 --> 00:02:42.023
The Leonard N. Stern School
Of Business graduates,

00:02:42.023 --> 00:02:43.212
they're covered.

00:02:43.212 --> 00:02:45.444
The school of medicine graduates,

00:02:45.444 --> 00:02:48.163
each one will get a job.

00:02:48.163 --> 00:02:50.853
The proud graduates of
the NY School Of Law,

00:02:50.853 --> 00:02:52.577
they're covered.

00:02:52.577 --> 00:02:53.651
And, if they're not, who cares?

00:02:53.651 --> 00:02:54.910
They're lawyers.

00:02:54.910 --> 00:02:58.049
(audience laughs)

00:02:58.049 --> 00:03:00.255
The English majors, are not a factor.

00:03:00.255 --> 00:03:03.088
(audience laughs)

00:03:04.664 --> 00:03:07.774
They'll be home, writing their novels.

00:03:07.774 --> 00:03:10.388
Teachers, they'll all be working.

00:03:10.388 --> 00:03:12.024
(bleep) jobs, lousy pay, yeah,

00:03:12.024 --> 00:03:13.966
but still working.

00:03:13.966 --> 00:03:15.576
The graduates in accounting,

00:03:15.576 --> 00:03:17.768
they all have jobs.

00:03:17.768 --> 00:03:19.680
Where does that leave you?

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:21.772
Envious of those accountants?

00:03:21.772 --> 00:03:23.522
I doubt it.

00:03:23.522 --> 00:03:25.022
They had a choice.

00:03:26.005 --> 00:03:27.914
Maybe they were passionate
about accounting,

00:03:27.914 --> 00:03:29.126
but I think it's more likely

00:03:29.126 --> 00:03:31.200
that they used reason, and logic,

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.620
and common sense, to reach for a career,

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:36.821
(audience laughs)
that could give them

00:03:36.821 --> 00:03:40.238
the expectation of success and stability.

00:03:41.498 --> 00:03:44.229
Reason, logic, common sense,

00:03:44.229 --> 00:03:45.927
at the TISCH School Of Arts?

00:03:45.927 --> 00:03:47.520
(audience laughs)

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:49.103
Are you kidding me?

00:03:50.193 --> 00:03:52.891
But, you didn't have that choice, did you?

00:03:52.891 --> 00:03:54.072
You discovered a talent,

00:03:54.072 --> 00:03:57.401
developed an ambition and
recognized your passion.

00:03:57.401 --> 00:03:59.517
When you feel that, you can't fight it.

00:03:59.517 --> 00:04:01.053
You just go with it.

00:04:01.053 --> 00:04:02.294
When it comes to the arts,

00:04:02.294 --> 00:04:04.804
passion should always trump, common sense.

00:04:04.804 --> 00:04:06.937
You aren't just following dreams,

00:04:06.937 --> 00:04:09.043
you're reaching for your destiny.

00:04:09.043 --> 00:04:11.600
You're a dancer, a
singer, a choreographer,

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:13.498
a musician, a film maker, a writer,

00:04:13.498 --> 00:04:15.802
a photographer, a director, a producer,

00:04:15.802 --> 00:04:17.469
an actor, an artist.

00:04:19.396 --> 00:04:20.549
Yeah, you're (bleep).

00:04:20.549 --> 00:04:22.485
(audience laughs)

00:04:22.485 --> 00:04:23.933
The good news is,

00:04:23.933 --> 00:04:27.782
that, that's not a bad place to start.

00:04:27.782 --> 00:04:29.579
Now that you've made your choice,

00:04:29.579 --> 00:04:31.446
or rather succumbed to it,

00:04:31.446 --> 00:04:33.029
your path is clear.

00:04:33.933 --> 00:04:36.161
Not easy, but clear.

00:04:36.161 --> 00:04:39.035
You have to keep working,
it's that simple.

00:04:39.035 --> 00:04:41.165
- How did you get into entrepreneurship?

00:04:41.165 --> 00:04:43.389
Was it something that when you were young,

00:04:43.389 --> 00:04:45.475
you got inspired about it, or

00:04:45.475 --> 00:04:47.185
because I know your father was an artist,

00:04:47.185 --> 00:04:48.340
and your mother, as well.

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:50.724
- Yeah, I don't know.

00:04:50.724 --> 00:04:54.307
I've always been wanting
to act, or direct.

00:04:57.145 --> 00:04:59.054
But, then, these opportunities came along,

00:04:59.054 --> 00:05:00.196
and I felt,

00:05:00.196 --> 00:05:01.661
"Let's just take advantage of them."

00:05:01.661 --> 00:05:02.578
Like, Nobu.

00:05:04.231 --> 00:05:06.398
I went to Nobu, years ago,

00:05:07.394 --> 00:05:10.061
over 20 years ago, 25 years ago.

00:05:11.824 --> 00:05:14.642
I went to Matsuhisa, his first restaurant,

00:05:14.642 --> 00:05:15.475
I said,

00:05:15.475 --> 00:05:16.357
"If you ever want to open a restaurant."

00:05:16.357 --> 00:05:18.887
I was there, one time and then I went

00:05:18.887 --> 00:05:19.787
many times after.

00:05:19.787 --> 00:05:20.620
I said,

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:21.836
"If you ever want to open a restaurant

00:05:21.836 --> 00:05:23.734
"in New York, just let me know."

00:05:23.734 --> 00:05:26.931
Because, to me, it was a no-brainer.

00:05:26.931 --> 00:05:27.985
- [Narrator] And he set the bar

00:05:27.985 --> 00:05:29.429
for a crazy father in-law,

00:05:29.429 --> 00:05:30.878
along side, Ben Stiller

00:05:30.878 --> 00:05:33.197
and the Focker clan.

00:05:33.197 --> 00:05:34.635
- Alright, now look, Focker.

00:05:34.635 --> 00:05:35.786
I'm a patient man,

00:05:35.786 --> 00:05:37.597
that's what 19 months in
a Vietnamese prison camp

00:05:37.597 --> 00:05:38.830
will do to you.

00:05:38.830 --> 00:05:41.349
But, I will be watching you,

00:05:41.349 --> 00:05:43.245
studying your every move.

00:05:43.245 --> 00:05:45.523
- [Narrator] While they
were a commercial success,

00:05:45.523 --> 00:05:46.829
some critics lamented that,

00:05:46.829 --> 00:05:48.911
they were trivial roles.

00:05:48.911 --> 00:05:52.258
Beneath an actor of his stature.

00:05:52.258 --> 00:05:54.263
- That's fine, they can criticize,

00:05:54.263 --> 00:05:56.677
and they could be right.

00:05:56.677 --> 00:05:58.829
I want to do those things.

00:05:58.829 --> 00:06:01.613
You can't please everybody, all the time,

00:06:01.613 --> 00:06:03.323
or even part of the time.

00:06:03.323 --> 00:06:05.949
- Are you a good picker
of scripts and things,

00:06:05.949 --> 00:06:09.039
or do you get somebody else to help you?

00:06:09.039 --> 00:06:10.486
- Well, you get people to help you

00:06:10.486 --> 00:06:11.708
in terms of getting feedback.

00:06:11.708 --> 00:06:13.986
I have people like, my agent or

00:06:13.986 --> 00:06:15.710
people who work with me,

00:06:15.710 --> 00:06:18.959
they will read it, read the script

00:06:18.959 --> 00:06:21.065
and tell me if they think it's

00:06:21.065 --> 00:06:22.689
if it's good.

00:06:22.689 --> 00:06:25.311
Because, I'm so busy, I don't have time to

00:06:25.311 --> 00:06:26.783
read everything that comes along.

00:06:26.783 --> 00:06:30.864
Sometimes, it's just
something that I can see

00:06:30.864 --> 00:06:33.856
in a script that, maybe no one else can,

00:06:33.856 --> 00:06:37.695
and I follow my own instincts about it.

00:06:37.695 --> 00:06:40.214
- Why, at this stage, did you decide

00:06:40.214 --> 00:06:41.950
to direct a picture?

00:06:41.950 --> 00:06:44.058
Was it because the
particular subject matter

00:06:44.058 --> 00:06:46.691
or because you had a yearning to direct?

00:06:46.691 --> 00:06:48.256
- Well, I always wanted to direct.

00:06:48.256 --> 00:06:49.173
Since I was

00:06:51.151 --> 00:06:53.186
in my late teens, early 20s,

00:06:53.186 --> 00:06:54.653
and I just never got around to it.

00:06:54.653 --> 00:06:57.254
I finally started realizing
as I was getting older,

00:06:57.254 --> 00:07:01.391
by my early 40s that
I better really do it.

00:07:01.391 --> 00:07:03.795
It's either now or I'm
never going to do it.

00:07:03.795 --> 00:07:05.571
- What is the one thing, that you want

00:07:05.571 --> 00:07:08.404
people to think or know about you?

00:07:10.854 --> 00:07:12.690
- I don't know.

00:07:12.690 --> 00:07:15.541
Whatever, I mean, you know I try my best.

00:07:15.541 --> 00:07:17.208
- Don't give a damn.

00:07:18.146 --> 00:07:19.305
You don't give a damn,

00:07:19.305 --> 00:07:20.138
about what people think?

00:07:20.138 --> 00:07:21.685
- I mean, I care, but I don't let it...

00:07:21.685 --> 00:07:24.730
I try to do my best at
doing the right thing,

00:07:24.730 --> 00:07:25.638
if you will.

00:07:25.638 --> 00:07:27.805
Not always, but I hope I'm

00:07:29.151 --> 00:07:30.918
perceived as that or

00:07:30.918 --> 00:07:32.253
appreciated for that.

00:07:32.253 --> 00:07:34.514
It's difficult for me to
come up with advice for you

00:07:34.514 --> 00:07:36.527
who have already set
upon your life's work.

00:07:36.527 --> 00:07:37.677
But, I can tell you some of the things,

00:07:37.677 --> 00:07:38.918
I tell my own children.

00:07:38.918 --> 00:07:40.800
First, whatever you do,

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:42.406
don't go to TISCH School Of The Arts.

00:07:42.406 --> 00:07:45.416
(audience laughs)

00:07:45.416 --> 00:07:48.494
Get an accounting degree, instead.

00:07:48.494 --> 00:07:50.304
Then, I contradict myself,

00:07:50.304 --> 00:07:51.868
and as corny as it sounds, I tell them,

00:07:51.868 --> 00:07:53.318
"Don't be afraid to fail."

00:07:53.318 --> 00:07:55.704
I urge them to take chances,
to keep an open mind,

00:07:55.704 --> 00:07:58.564
to welcome new experiences and new ideas.

00:07:58.564 --> 00:08:01.950
I tell them that, if you don't go,

00:08:01.950 --> 00:08:03.495
you'll never know.

00:08:03.495 --> 00:08:05.751
You have to have the,

00:08:05.751 --> 00:08:07.627
you just have to be bold and go out there

00:08:07.627 --> 00:08:08.947
and take your chances.

00:08:08.947 --> 00:08:10.963
What I like is if somebody says,

00:08:10.963 --> 00:08:13.180
"I never thought of you
for this type of part,

00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:15.034
"but then I thought, yeah.

00:08:15.034 --> 00:08:17.065
"you would be good for it,
just for that very reason."

00:08:17.065 --> 00:08:19.338
That would spark something in me

00:08:19.338 --> 00:08:20.635
that makes it different.

00:08:20.635 --> 00:08:22.617
You and I were even talking
a little bit about that.

00:08:22.617 --> 00:08:25.011
You mentioned some things.

00:08:25.011 --> 00:08:28.563
Sometimes, you need that
from an outside source

00:08:28.563 --> 00:08:31.999
in order to stimulate you
and get you inspired almost,

00:08:31.999 --> 00:08:35.749
or revved up about doing
something different.

00:08:36.913 --> 00:08:39.304
- Thank you guys so much, for watching.

00:08:39.304 --> 00:08:41.302
I made this video because, jurgen803,

00:08:41.302 --> 00:08:42.393
asked me to.

00:08:42.393 --> 00:08:43.552
So, if there's a famous entreprenuer

00:08:43.552 --> 00:08:45.130
that you want me to profile next,

00:08:45.130 --> 00:08:46.649
leave it in the comments below

00:08:46.649 --> 00:08:47.862
and I'll see what I can do.

00:08:47.862 --> 00:08:48.914
I'd also love to know

00:08:48.914 --> 00:08:50.545
which of Robert Deniro's top 10 rules,

00:08:50.545 --> 00:08:52.998
had the biggest impact on you and why.

00:08:52.998 --> 00:08:53.909
Leave it in the comments and

00:08:53.909 --> 00:08:55.838
I will join in the discussion.

00:08:55.838 --> 00:08:57.167
Thank you so much for watching.

00:08:57.167 --> 00:09:00.750
Continue to believe,
and I'll see you soon.

00:09:05.888 --> 00:09:06.988
- Do you remember the audition

00:09:06.988 --> 00:09:09.217
when you first met, Deniro?

00:09:09.217 --> 00:09:11.473
- That was one of those,

00:09:11.473 --> 00:09:13.272
incredible auditions that came around

00:09:13.272 --> 00:09:15.452
that I suppose every kid had been

00:09:15.452 --> 00:09:17.342
waiting for, for a long period of time,

00:09:17.342 --> 00:09:20.006
because it's just not that often

00:09:20.006 --> 00:09:23.594
that you get a starring
role above Robert Deniro,

00:09:23.594 --> 00:09:26.482
in this incredible screenplay.

00:09:26.482 --> 00:09:28.609
Ellen Barkin and Michael Caton-Jones,

00:09:28.609 --> 00:09:30.469
it was just, everyone was sort of

00:09:30.469 --> 00:09:32.386
fiending for this role.

00:09:33.551 --> 00:09:35.468
I got down, I suppose to the final five,

00:09:35.468 --> 00:09:36.521
and there was like,

00:09:36.521 --> 00:09:38.673
a last, sort of, day.

00:09:38.673 --> 00:09:40.531
Toby was there and I,

00:09:40.531 --> 00:09:43.605
I do remember thinking to myself,

00:09:43.605 --> 00:09:44.891
"I got to do something.

00:09:44.891 --> 00:09:47.698
"I got to do something to stand out."

00:09:47.698 --> 00:09:49.137
In the audition, there was

00:09:49.137 --> 00:09:50.841
Art Linson and Michael Caton-Jones.

00:09:50.841 --> 00:09:54.126
Art Linson was the producer and Deniro.

00:09:54.126 --> 00:09:55.376
And, I remember

00:09:56.264 --> 00:09:59.071
it was a mustard jar, scene.

00:09:59.071 --> 00:10:00.413
And, he had to jam a mustard jar,

00:10:00.413 --> 00:10:02.521
in my eye, repeatedly.

00:10:02.521 --> 00:10:04.179
It was an abuse scene.

00:10:04.179 --> 00:10:06.569
And the script didn't call for it,

00:10:06.569 --> 00:10:08.601
but I got up and he said,

00:10:08.601 --> 00:10:09.836
"Is it empty, is it empty?"

00:10:09.836 --> 00:10:10.879
And, I got up and I screamed,

00:10:10.879 --> 00:10:11.712
"No!"

00:10:12.921 --> 00:10:14.677
Like, completely unnecessary,

00:10:14.677 --> 00:10:16.755
but I screamed it in his face.

00:10:16.755 --> 00:10:19.476
And, I sat there, you know,

00:10:19.476 --> 00:10:20.309
with my head, you know,

00:10:20.309 --> 00:10:21.840
looking like a red tomato,

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:25.351
and everyone started laughing at me,

00:10:25.351 --> 00:10:26.444
in the entire room.

00:10:26.444 --> 00:10:28.650
Deniro, everybody was in hysterics

00:10:28.650 --> 00:10:30.054
and I sat there frozen thinking,

00:10:30.054 --> 00:10:31.270
"Oh my God, I just

00:10:31.270 --> 00:10:34.742
"screwed this entire opportunity up."

00:10:34.742 --> 00:10:36.284
And, I just sat there frozen

00:10:36.284 --> 00:10:37.954
and then, you know, Bob in

00:10:37.954 --> 00:10:39.529
traditional Deniro fashion,

00:10:39.529 --> 00:10:40.976
kind of just, looked at me and went,

00:10:40.976 --> 00:10:42.795
"It's good, it's good."

00:10:42.795 --> 00:10:45.628
(audience laughs)

00:10:48.745 --> 00:10:51.339
And, then, we carried on doing the scene,

00:10:51.339 --> 00:10:54.134
and I think, I actually had dinner

00:10:54.134 --> 00:10:56.641
with Deniro recently and he told me,

00:10:56.641 --> 00:10:59.168
"I was the one who said,

00:10:59.168 --> 00:11:01.251
"you should get the job."

00:11:23.865 --> 00:11:26.845
- Faster than you, (bleep).

00:11:26.845 --> 00:11:30.162
Saw you coming, you (bleep).

00:11:30.162 --> 00:11:32.162
(bleep)

